    <summary>One native desktop home for your manga and comics, books and audiobooks, movies, shows, and anime.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
## Colosseum

**A native desktop media environment for manga and comics, books and audiobooks, movies, shows, and anime.**

Your media library usually lives in five different apps: one for manga, one for comics, one for
books, one for audiobooks, one for film and TV. Each has its own catalogue, its own reader or
player, its own idea of progress — and none of them talk to each other.

Colosseum is one fullscreen home for all of it. Three worlds share a single shell:

- **Tankoban** — manga and western comics, with a real comic reader.
- **Biblio** — ebooks and audiobooks, with a real book reader and audiobook read-along.
- **Theatre** — movies, shows, and anime, with a real video player.

They share one Continue row across every medium, one Collection, one Downloads surface, one taskbar
of open sessions, and **Vault**, a local-media library for files you already own. Browsing is
catalogue-first and discovery-rich; downloaded media stays local so reading and listening continue
offline.

### Install notes

- **Per-user install, no administrator required.** Colosseum installs into
  `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Colosseum` for the account that runs the installer, because the app
  writes its catalogs, download index, and cache beside itself. When Chocolatey runs elevated, the
  install therefore lands under the elevated account's profile — run `choco install colosseum` from
  the shell of the user who will actually use the app if that matters for your setup.
- **Windows 10/11 desktop only.**
- Desktop and Start-menu shortcuts are created automatically.
- Uninstalling removes the whole Colosseum folder, **including downloaded media and reading
  progress** (this is by design — the library lives inside the install folder in this release).

### Verification

The installer is downloaded directly from the official GitHub release and verified by SHA-256
checksum. See `tools\VERIFICATION.txt` in the package for details.
]]></description>
